            <!-- Rich Text Body -->
            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'zh'">
                <p>某跨境电商团队每月底都会收到Meta、Google和TikTok广告平台的账单，但财务系统里只有充值记录，没有逐笔消耗和发票状态。复盘时，运营发现一笔约18,640.67元的Google Ads付款既没有完整关联到广告账户，也找不到对应发票；另有几笔Meta消耗被重复记入“投放费用”。问题看起来只是金额对不上，实际涉及账户归属、币种汇率、平台税费和发票状态四个环节。</p>
<h2>先用一张表找到差异，而不是逐笔翻账单</h2>
<p>团队把问题整理为“平台—账户—币种—付款—消耗—发票”六列台账，并将三个数据源拉到一起：</p>
<ul>
<li>付款记录：银行流水、信用卡账单及以渔充值订单；</li>
<li>消耗记录：各广告平台后台的账单、费用明细与余额变动；</li>
<li>财务记录：记账凭证、付款审批和待认证发票。</li>
</ul>
<p>判断差异时，不能直接拿“充值金额”与“广告消耗”相减。充值是资金进入平台，消耗是账户实际使用，两者之间还可能存在退款、赠金、预扣税、账户余额和汇率波动。</p>
<p>例如，上述18,640.67元付款进入Google Ads后，账户实际消耗可能低于付款额，剩余部分形成账户余额。若财务直接记为当期投放费用，就会造成费用跨期；若余额长期挂账，又会掩盖账户闲置或内部转账问题。</p>
<h2>统一处理动作：付款、消耗、余额分开记</h2>
<h3>1. 固定账户与币种映射</h3>
<p>每个广告账户都绑定内部成本中心、平台、币种、付款主体和负责人。涉及多账户投放时，应在台账中保留“付款账户”和“消耗账户”的对应关系，避免把A账户充值直接认定为B账户消耗。</p>
<p>币种也必须明确。人民币付款、美元充值和当地账户扣款不能混成一个数字。建议同时记录原币金额、付款金额、记账汇率和汇率日期；财务记账采用统一口径，投放复盘则使用平台原币数据。</p>
<h3>2. 每次充值都生成资金流水</h3>
<p>充值前记录计划金额、付款方式和目标账户；到账后核对平台余额，并标记“未消耗、已消耗、已退款或待结算”。以渔的充值订单可以作为付款凭证索引，再关联银行或信用卡流水，形成可追踪链路。</p>
<p>对于预充值广告账户，可按实际消耗确认当期费用，将账户余额作为预付款资产处理。若企业采用平台消耗直接扣款的方式，则应将账单中的投放费用、平台服务费和税费分列。</p>
<h3>3. 用差异表锁定三类异常</h3>
<p>团队最终保留以下核对规则：</p>
<ul>
<li>付款已到账，但未关联目标账户：列为资金归属异常；</li>
<li>平台有消耗，但财务没有费用记录：列为漏记或跨期异常；</li>
<li>已取得发票，但金额、税号或抬头不匹配：列为认证异常。</li>
</ul>
<p>平台账单与内部台账可设置合理差异阈值，但税费、赠金和汇率造成的差异应单独解释，不能简单归入“尾差”。</p>
<h2>发票不是附在付款后面，而是对账的一部分</h2>
<p>月末对账建议按以下顺序执行：</p>
<ol>
<li>导出各平台当期费用明细和余额清单；</li>
<li>与充值订单、付款流水逐笔匹配；</li>
<li>按广告账户汇总实际消耗，核对财务费用；</li>
<li>检查退款、返点、税费和赠金；</li>
<li>将发票抬头、税号、金额、日期及状态关联到对应付款；</li>
<li>对异常项指定负责人和处理期限。</li>
</ol>
<p>发票缺失时，先确认平台是否已开票、是否达到开票门槛或是否需要客户资料补全。金额不符时，应回到原币账单判断是汇率问题、税费问题还是平台调整，而不是直接要求业务重新提交一张发票。</p>
<h2>对账完成后的结果</h2>
<p>调整后，团队不再以“充值金额等于投放金额”作为判断标准，而是形成三张互相勾稽的表：资金流水表、账户消耗表和发票匹配表。每月对账时，财务先看付款是否入账，投放负责人再看消耗是否进入正确账户，业务最后确认发票是否齐全。</p>
<p>复盘发现，那笔未完整关联的Google Ads付款最终对应到正确的广告账户和内部成本中心；部分Meta重复费用被重新归类；另有若干余额被从长期未核销状态转入可追踪的预付款记录。团队没有一次性解决所有历史问题，而是先完成账户映射和本期账单核对，再按金额与账龄逐步清理旧差异。</p>
<p>这套SOP的核心并不是增加财务工作，而是让每笔海外广告资金都能回答三个问题：钱付到了哪里、投放实际消耗了多少、对应发票能否用于入账与认证。对于多账户、跨币种和月结账单并存的出海团队，这通常比单纯核对总金额更可靠。</p>
            </div>
            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <p>At the end of each month, a cross-border e-commerce team received statements from Meta, Google, and TikTok Ads. However, its finance system contained only top-up records, with no detailed reconciliation between payments, actual ad spend, balances, and invoice status. During a review, the team found that a Google Ads payment of approximately $18,640.67 had not been fully linked to an ad account or matched with an invoice. Several Meta expenses had also been duplicated in the accounting ledger. Although the issue appeared to be a simple amount mismatch, it involved four areas: account ownership, currency conversion, platform taxes, and invoice status.</p>
<h2>Start with a unified table to identify where the differences originate</h2>
<p>The team built a six-column reconciliation sheet: platform, account, currency, payment, spend, and invoice. It consolidated three data sources:</p>
<ul>
<li>Payment records, including bank statements, credit card statements, and ad account top-up orders;</li>
<li>Spend records, including platform statements, transaction-level charges, and balance changes;</li>
<li>Finance records, including journal entries, payment approvals, and invoices pending verification.</li>
</ul>
<p>Top-up amounts should not be directly compared with advertising spend. A top-up adds funds to a platform, while spend represents the amount actually used. Refunds, promotional credits, withholding taxes, account balances, and exchange-rate movements can also affect the difference.</p>
<p>In the example, the $18,640.67 payment was credited to Google Ads, but the actual amount spent was lower. The remainder became an account balance. If finance recorded the entire payment as current-period advertising expense, the result would be period allocation errors. Leaving the balance unmonitored would also hide idle funds or internal transfer issues.</p>
<h2>Separate payments, spend, balances, and invoices</h2>
<h3>1. Establish a fixed account and currency mapping</h3>
<p>Each ad account should be linked to an internal cost center, platform, currency, legal entity, and owner. For multi-account operations, the ledger must preserve the relationship between the funded account and the account that actually incurred the spend. A top-up to Account A should not automatically be treated as spend in Account B.</p>
<p>Currencies must also be standardized. Record the original currency amount, payment amount, accounting exchange rate, and rate date. Finance can use a consistent accounting method, while campaign analysis should rely on the platform's original-currency data.</p>
<h3>2. Create a transaction record for every top-up</h3>
<p>Before funding an account, record the planned amount, payment method, and target account. After the funds arrive, verify the platform balance and mark the funds as unspent, spent, refunded, or pending settlement. The top-up order can serve as a payment index and be linked with bank or card statements to create a traceable transaction chain.</p>
<p>For prepaid accounts, recognize advertising expense based on actual consumption and report the remaining balance as a prepaid asset. If the platform deducts charges directly, separate advertising spend, platform fees, and taxes in the accounting entries.</p>
<h3>3. Flag three types of exceptions</h3>
<p>The team adopted three reconciliation rules:</p>
<ul>
<li>Funds received but not linked to the intended account: an account allocation exception;</li>
<li>Spend recorded by the platform but absent from the general ledger: a missing or period allocation exception;</li>
<li>Invoice amount, tax ID, or customer name does not match the payment: a tax invoice compliance exception.</li>
</ul>
<p>Reasonable tolerance thresholds may be used, but differences caused by taxes, promotional credits, or exchange rates should be explained separately rather than dismissed as rounding errors.</p>
<h2>Treat invoices as part of reconciliation, not as an afterthought</h2>
<p>The month-end process follows a clear sequence:</p>
<ol>
<li>Export detailed charges and balance reports from each ad platform;</li>
<li>Match them with top-up orders and payment statements;</li>
<li>Aggregate actual spend by ad account and compare it with finance expenses;</li>
<li>Review refunds, rebates, taxes, and promotional credits;</li>
<li>Link invoice header, tax ID, amount, date, and verification status to the relevant payment;</li>
<li>Assign an owner and deadline to every exception.</li>
</ol>
<p>When an invoice is missing, first confirm whether the platform has issued it, whether the minimum billing threshold has been reached, or whether customer information needs to be updated. If the amount does not match, review the original-currency statement to determine whether the difference is related to exchange rates, taxes, or a platform adjustment.</p>
<h2>The result: three connected records instead of one amount check</h2>
<p>After the adjustment, the team stopped using “top-up amount equals ad spend” as its reconciliation standard. It created three connected records: a cash movement ledger, an account spend ledger, and an invoice matching register. Finance first checks whether funds have been recorded, the media team confirms whether spend is assigned to the correct account, and the business owner verifies whether invoices are complete and compliant.</p>
<p>The unmatched Google Ads payment was eventually linked to the correct ad account and internal cost center. Duplicate Meta expenses were reclassified, and several long-outstanding balances were moved into a traceable prepaid balance. Rather than attempting to resolve every historical issue at once, the team first standardized account mappings and reconciled the current period, then cleared older discrepancies based on value and age.</p>
<p>The purpose of this SOP is not to create more work for finance. It ensures that every overseas advertising payment can answer three questions: where the money was sent, how much was actually spent, and whether the invoice can be recognized and used for tax verification. For businesses managing multiple accounts, currencies, and monthly billing cycles, this is more reliable than comparing only the total amounts.</p>
